          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. State whether the following sentences are True or False. Justify your answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.148,
            0.849,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) A linear equation in two variables has only one solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.168,
            0.881,
            0.207
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) The graph of a linear equation in two variables always passes through the origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.206,
            0.881,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) A linear equation in two variables can never have rational solutions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.244,
            0.751,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) x = 3 is a valid linear equation in two variables."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.263,
            0.824,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(v) The equation 2x + 3y = 7 has infinitely many solutions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.283,
            0.828,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(vi) The point (1, 2) is a solution of the equation 2x + 3y = 7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.312,
            0.883,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. (i) Compare the solutions of the equations 3x + 4y = 7 and 6x + 8y = 14. Argue that they have the same set of solutions, that is, every solution of one is also a solution of the other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.37,
            0.881,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Show that the equations ax + by = c and kax + kby = kc, with k ≠ 0, have the same set of solutions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.435,
            0.797,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.3 Slope-intercept form of a Linear Equation in Two Variables"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.493,
            0.461,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.3.1 Understanding Slope"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.525,
            0.473,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Have you ever walked or cycled up a steep hill? How did it feel compared to walking on a flat road? On a flat road, you can move without much effort. But as the road becomes steeper, you have to work harder to climb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.66,
            0.47,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The steeper it is, the more effort is needed to cycle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.698,
            0.471,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In mathematics, the idea of ‘steepness’ of a line is called its "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "slope",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "gradient",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.757,
            0.47,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "positive slope",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " means going ‘upward’ as we move from left to right."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.82,
            0.471,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "negative slope",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " means going ‘downward’ as we move from left to right."
            }
          ]
        },
